{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underestimation: when the last 10–30 days show a rising regime, the model level is too low almost everywhere. Apply an additive uplift that grows with horizon (small at days 1–3, large by days 10–30).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors increase with lead time (near-term misses are modest; 2–4 week forecasts are far too low). Use a ramping correction (e.g., linear or convex increase in uplift vs. forecast day).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the forecast path rises too slowly and often flattens or dips while the realized series keeps climbing. Add a momentum term based on recent slope/acceleration (e.g., last 5–10 day return) to steepen the trajectory.",
    "False mean reversion after breakouts: after a price breaks higher, the model repeatedly predicts pullbacks/sideways action that do not occur. Suppress mean-reversion logic when price is making higher highs (e.g., above recent 20-day high or positive 10-day slope).",
    "Volatility/step-change underreaction: realized moves include step-ups (multi-day jumps) that the model smooths away. In high-momentum regimes, widen the forecast distribution upward (or add jump-risk premium) rather than smoothing toward the prior mean."
  ]
}
